  "name": "Question 13",
  "problem": "Which of the following relations are reflexive? \n\n- $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $(x+y)∣10,$ defined on $Z$\n\n- $x\\,S\\,y$ if and only if $4∣({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}),$ defined on $Z$\n\n- $x\\,T\\,y$ if and only if $x∣(y+1),$ defined on $Z$",
  "worked_solution": "A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is \n\n- *reflexive* if $(x,x)\\in R$ for all $x\\in A,$\n\n- *irreflexive* if $(x,x)\\notin R$ for all $x\\in A.$\n\nIf some pairs of the form $(x,x)$ belong to a relation and others do not, then the relation is neither reflexive nor irreflexive.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our relations.\n\n- Relation $R$ is neither reflexive nor irreflexive. Notice that \n\n- $(2,2)\\notin R$ since $2+2=4$ and $4∤10,$ while\n\n- $(1,1)\\in R$ since $1+1=2$ and $2∣10.$\n\n- Relation $S$ is reflexive. Indeed, $(x,x)\\in S$ for all $x\\in Z.$ Notice that \n\n$$\n4∣({x}^{2}-{x}^{2})\n$$\n\nfor any $x\\in Z.$\n\n- Relation $T$ is neither reflexive nor irreflexive. Notice that \n\n- $(2,2)\\notin T$ since $2∤(2+1),$ while\n\n- $(1,1)\\in T$ since $1∣(1+1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$S$ only.\"",
